  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Lab Results Report",
    "overview": "\n**Creative North Star: \"The Lab Results Report\"**\n\nEach pain arrives as a result, not a card: its evidence measured against the reference ranges the weekly run itself used, with what falls in or out of range said in words. The page reads like a clinical report on white paper: an institutional blue band across the top, pale blue-grey section heads, hairline-ruled tables, ink on white. At night (automatic, no toggle) it is the same report on a deep navy ground, never graphite and never warm.\n\nThe voice is calm, exact and unadorned. Density is that of a document a person reads closely on Monday, on a phone or a computer: small tracked section labels, tabular numbers, short plain-Spanish sentences. Nothing decorates; the one drawn element is the reference-range bar, because it is data.\n\nThis replaces the earlier andon-board world, which was rejected, above all for its yellow. None of that world carries over.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "White paper, one institutional blue, hairline rules; flat, no shadows.",
      "One typeface (Public Sans) with tabular figures everywhere.",
      "Never yellow or amber. Attention is blue.",
      "Every state is written in words beside its colour; dots and bars are `aria-hidden`.",
      "The signature move is the reference-range bar drawn from the run's real thresholds.",
      "AA contrast in both themes, usable from 320 px."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Never-Yellow Rule",
        "body": "No yellow, amber or orange anywhere, in either theme. Attention is blue. (The code keeps the internal attribute name `data-lamp=\"amber\"`; it is only a name and renders the attention blue. Likewise `green`, `red`, `off`.)",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Words-Beside-Colour Rule",
        "body": "A state is never colour alone. The dot or bar is `aria-hidden` and the state is written next to it («DENTRO DEL RANGO», «FUERA DEL RANGO», the run's status).",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Text-Variant Rule",
        "body": "A state written as text uses its `-text` value (4.5:1 or better); the plain state value is for dots, bars and control edges (3:1). A green dot never sits directly on the section tint: it goes inside a chip on the panel.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The No-Eyebrow Rule",
        "body": "Tracked uppercase labels name a section or a table; they never sit as a kicker above a heading.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Out-of-Range-Is-Bold Rule",
        "body": "A value outside its range is set bold in the failing state's text colour, as a lab report marks it.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Hairline-Not-Shadow Rule",
        "body": "Separate surfaces with a 1px Hairline or a Section Tint head, never a shadow.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "write every state in words beside its colour; keep dots and bars `aria-hidden`.",
      "draw ranges from the run's real thresholds and flag them in words.",
      "use Public Sans at 400/500/600/700 with tabular figures.",
      "separate surfaces with 1px Hairline rules and the Section Tint; keep radii 2 to 4px.",
      "keep both themes at AA (4.5:1 text, 3:1 marks and control edges) and usable at 320 px."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "use yellow, amber or orange, or reintroduce the andon-board look (lamps, graphite, a second display face).",
      "convey a state by colour alone.",
      "add shadows, gradients or rounded pills.",
      "put a tracked uppercase label above a heading as an eyebrow.",
      "show jargon or internal ids; the page is plain Spanish."
    ]
  }
}